The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (shm_may_destroy(shp)) {
    shm_lock_by_ptr(shp);
    shm_destroy(ns, shp);
}
Reason:
The function shm_may_destroy() reads shp->shm_nattch, which can be updated by concurrent attach/detach operations while holding only shm_perm.lock (not the rwsem). In the original code, shm_may_destroy() is called before acquiring shm_perm.lock, allowing a race condition where the segment appears unused and is destroyed, while another thread has already incremented shm_nattch but not yet made it visible. This leads to premature destruction of a shared memory segment that is still in use.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
shm_lock_by_ptr(shp);
if (shm_may_destroy(shp))
    shm_destroy(ns, shp);
else
    shm_unlock(shp);
Reason:
By moving shm_lock_by_ptr() (which acquires shm_perm.lock) before the call to shm_may_destroy(), the check is now performed under the same lock that protects shm_nattch updates. This serializes the test with concurrent attach/detach operations, preventing the race. If the segment is not actually ready for destruction (e.g., shm_nattch > 0), the lock is released via shm_unlock() instead of proceeding with destruction.

